.gitignore only affects untracked files. It does not automatically remove files that Git has already started tracking or staging.

If an interviewer asks:
Does git add create objects?
A strong answer is:
"Yes. When I run git add, Git computes a hash of the file content, compresses it, and stores it as a blob object in .git/objects. The index (.git/index) records which object hashes are staged. When I later run git commit, Git creates tree and commit objects that organize those blobs into a snapshot, then updates the current branch and HEAD to point to the new commit."
I think you'll enjoy the next step
Since you're interested in internals, we can actually inspect your .git directory live.
We'll run commands like:
git cat-file -t <hash>
git cat-file -p <hash>
git ls-files --stage
git ls-tree
git rev-parse HEAD

You'll see your actual blob objects, tree objects, commit objects, and the index, not just diagrams. This is one of the best ways to build a deep understanding of Git for senior-level interviews.